1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,420
1902.

2
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:03,698
A leaky shed at a Paris science school.

3
00:00:03,917 --> 00:00:09,480
For nearly four years, Marie Curie has
been processing tons of ore here, by hand.

4
00:00:09,667 --> 00:00:11,738
One night, she turns down the lamp.

5
00:00:15,917 --> 00:00:17,318
Her radium is glowing.

6
00:00:21,417 --> 00:00:27,437
It began in 1896, when Henri Becquerel
found that uranium gives off invisible

7
00:00:27,437 --> 00:00:27,828
rays.

8
00:00:28,042 --> 00:00:32,772
Curie measured them with an electrometer,
which detects the tiny current the rays

9
00:00:32,772 --> 00:00:33,590
create in air.

10
00:00:33,792 --> 00:00:37,980
The strength depended only on how much
uranium was present.

11
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:41,245
Wet or dry, powder or solid, it didn't
matter.

12
00:00:41,458 --> 00:00:44,296
So the rays came from the atoms
themselves.

13
00:00:44,296 --> 00:00:46,078
She named it radioactivity.

14
00:00:46,292 --> 00:00:51,169
But pitchblende, a uranium ore, was more
radioactive than its uranium could

15
00:00:51,169 --> 00:00:51,690
explain.

16
00:00:51,875 --> 00:00:53,844
Something else was inside.

17
00:00:53,844 --> 00:00:57,403
In 1898, the Curies announced two new
elements:

18
00:00:57,403 --> 00:01:03,082
polonium, for Marie's homeland, Poland,
and radium, from the Latin for ray.

19
00:01:03,292 --> 00:01:06,573
Proving radium was real meant extracting
it from the ore.

20
00:01:06,573 --> 00:01:08,588
That's the work we saw in the shed.

21
00:01:08,792 --> 00:01:10,828
Today we know why it happens:

22
00:01:10,828 --> 00:01:16,585
atoms like radium have unstable nuclei,
and as they decay, they release radiation.

23
00:01:16,792 --> 00:01:19,454
Radioactivity comes from inside the atom.

24
00:01:19,454 --> 00:01:24,454
Curie proved it by measuring carefully,
and it earned her Nobel Prizes in two

25
00:01:24,454 --> 00:01:25,687
different sciences.
